(Regression test for bug #44: `write` is the first sink LANGUAGE.md's
 "Format Strings Everywhere" names, and a `{list}` or `{map}` part written
 through it put the collection's heap address into the file - an address
 that changed on every run, so the file's contents were not even stable.

 Acceptance: the bytes that reach a real file on disk are the same bytes
 Print produces, read back here to prove they landed.)

a list called flat is [1, 2, 3].
a map called person is {"name": "Ada", "born": 1815}.

(Straight to stdout through a file handle, beside the Print control.)
Print "stdout: {flat}".
Open a file at 1 for writing called console.
write "stdout: {flat}\n" to console.

(And to a real file, read back so the assertion is on the stored bytes.)
open a file for writing called sink at "./test_bug44_write.txt".
Write "{flat}\n" to sink.
Write "{person}\n" to sink.
Close sink.

open a file for reading called stored at "./test_bug44_write.txt".
a buffer called line is 256 bytes in size.
(`Read line` keeps the line's own newline, so print without adding another.)
Read line from stored into line.
Print line without newline.
Read line from stored into line.
Print line without newline.
Close stored.
Delete the file "./test_bug44_write.txt".
